`;
---

<LessonLayout
  title="L32 — Scaling Agents"
>

  <LessonHeader
    num="L32"
    title="Scaling Agents"
    subtitle="Why agent workloads demand horizontal scaling, the stateless worker architecture, message queues as backpressure buffers, three-tier state storage, and cost control under multi-tenant load."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      L24 covered deploying a single-instance NestJS agent with BullMQ queues. This lesson
      addresses what happens when one instance isn't enough — how to scale to many workers
      while keeping agent state consistent, costs controlled, and no single worker becoming
      a bottleneck or single point of failure.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Horizontal vs Vertical Scaling</h2>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Vertical (Scale Up)</th><th>Horizontal (Scale Out)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>How</td><td>Bigger machine (more CPU, RAM)</td><td>More machines running in parallel</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>Hard — hits hardware limits</td><td>None in theory — keep adding instances</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Failure mode</td><td>Single point of failure</td><td>Redundant — one node down, others continue</td></tr>
          <tr><td>State</td><td>Can keep state locally</td><td>Requires externalised, shared state</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost model</td><td>Expensive premium hardware</td><td>Many cheap commodity instances</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Why agents must scale horizontally">
      <p>
        Agent workloads are <strong>I/O-bound</strong>: most wall-clock time is spent waiting
        on LLM inference and tool API calls. A faster CPU doesn't make LLMs generate tokens
        faster. You need <em>more workers running in parallel</em>, not a bigger single machine.
        The architecture of resilience follows directly: stateless workers, decoupled state,
        fault tolerance by re-queuing.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Stateless Worker Pattern</h2>

    <p>
      The core constraint: any worker must be able to handle any step of any agent task.
      This means <strong>zero local state</strong> — all context lives in a shared external store.
    </p>

    <div class="principle-grid">
      <div class="prin-card">
        <div class="prin-title">Stateless Workers</div>
        <p>Workers fetch context, act, save results, and terminate. Any worker can pick up where another left off — including after a crash.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prin-card">
        <div class="prin-title">Externalised State</div>
        <p>No "sticky sessions" that lock a user to a specific server. Context lives in Redis/Postgres — fully shared across the pool.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prin-card">
        <div class="prin-title">Fault Tolerance</div>
        <p>If a worker crashes mid-thought, the job is re-queued and picked up by another worker. No work is lost.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="stateless-worker.ts" code={statelessWorkerCode} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Three-Tier State Storage</h2>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  W1[Worker A]:::worker --> H[(Hot Storage\\nRedis\\nSession context)]:::hot
  W2[Worker B]:::worker --> H
  W3[Worker C]:::worker --> H
  H --> WM[(Warm Storage\\nPostgres\\nDurable results)]:::warm
  WM --> C[(Cold Storage\\nS3\\nLarge artifacts)]:::cold

  classDef worker fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef hot    fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
  classDef warm   fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef cold   fill:#f0fdf4,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Technology</th><th>What goes here</th><th>Characteristics</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Hot</strong></td>
            <td>Redis (cluster mode)</td>
            <td>Ephemeral session context, in-flight reasoning state</td>
            <td>Sub-millisecond reads; use TTLs to auto-clean abandoned sessions; cluster for horizontal scale</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Warm</strong></td>
            <td>Postgres / NoSQL</td>
            <td>Durable user data, final results, checkpoint history</td>
            <td>Reliable; heavy read load → replicas; use PgBouncer for connection pooling (workers exhaust DB connections fast)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Cold</strong></td>
            <td>S3 / Object store</td>
            <td>Large artifacts: PDFs, images, long tool outputs</td>
            <td>Keeps DB lean; reference by URL in warm storage; very cheap at scale</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Connection pooling is not optional">
      <p>
        Each worker opens database connections. At 50 workers × 10 concurrent tasks each,
        that is 500 simultaneous Postgres connections — far exceeding the default limit.
        <strong>PgBouncer</strong> (or equivalent) is mandatory before you scale beyond a
        handful of workers.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Message Queues as Backpressure Buffers</h2>

    <p>
      Queues decouple rapid user input from slow, expensive agent execution. They are your
      main tool for absorbing traffic spikes without crashing workers or over-scaling
      infrastructure.
    </p>

    <div class="queue-features">
      <div class="qf-row">
        <div class="qf-name">Backpressure</div>
        <p>Absorbs traffic spikes — tasks queue up while workers drain at their own pace. No worker overload.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="qf-row">
        <div class="qf-name">Cost control</div>
        <p>Worker count directly limits max concurrent LLM calls. Set workers = your API rate limit budget, not your traffic peak.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="qf-row">
        <div class="qf-name">Priority lanes</div>
        <p>Separate queues for paid vs. free tiers. Premium users never wait behind free-tier spikes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="qf-row">
        <div class="qf-name">Dead letter queues</div>
        <p>Failed jobs after N retries move to a dead letter queue — inspectable and retryable without blocking the main queue.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Cost Control &amp; Fairness at Scale</h2>

    <p>
      LLM costs scale linearly with token usage. One runaway loop or one heavy tenant can
      drain the budget for everyone. These controls must be in place before you scale.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="cost-control.ts" code={costControlCode} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Control</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Protects against</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Token budgets</strong></td><td>Hard cap per tenant/user per time window, tracked in Redis</td><td>Runaway loops, unexpectedly expensive queries</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Concurrency limits</strong></td><td>Max parallel tasks per tenant (Redis counter)</td><td>One tenant monopolising all workers</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Fair scheduling</strong></td><td>Round-robin across tenants before starting new tasks</td><td>Noisy-neighbour effect</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Throttling</strong></td><td>Return 429 or queue position instead of over-scaling</td><td>Cost spikes from traffic bursts</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Tiering</strong></td><td>High-throughput infrastructure reserved for premium</td><td>Free tier degrading paid tier performance</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Scaling Best Practices</h2>

    <div class="bp-list">
      <div class="bp-row"><span class="bp-tag">Assume failure</span><p>LLMs time out, tools break. Design for automatic retry at the queue level — don't expect workers to be reliable.</p></div>
      <div class="bp-row"><span class="bp-tag">Graceful shutdowns</span><p>Workers must finish their current task before terminating. Sending SIGKILL to a thinking agent loses that task. Use SIGTERM + drain logic.</p></div>
      <div class="bp-row"><span class="bp-tag">Scale on queue metrics</span><p>Monitor queue depth and queue latency, not CPU. Agent workers idle on CPU while waiting on I/O — CPU is the wrong signal for scaling decisions.</p></div>
      <div class="bp-row"><span class="bp-tag">Externalise everything</span><p>Logs, state, and secrets must live outside the container. Any container that holds unique state is a single point of failure.</p></div>
      <div class="bp-row"><span class="bp-tag">Incremental rollout</span><p>Test scaling behaviour (and costs) under synthetic load before exposing real traffic. Surprises at scale are expensive surprises.</p></div>
    </div>

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'Why do agent workloads specifically benefit from horizontal rather than vertical scaling?',
        options: [
          'Agent code is too complex to run on a single machine',
          'LLM inference is I/O-bound — more machines in parallel matters more than a faster single machine',
          'Horizontal scaling is always cheaper regardless of workload type',
          'Vertical scaling is not supported by cloud providers for AI workloads',
        ],
        answer:  1,
        explain: 'Agents spend most of their time waiting on LLM inference and external API calls — I/O-bound work. A faster CPU doesn\'t speed up a remote LLM call. More workers running in parallel (horizontal) directly increases throughput. Vertical scaling only helps for CPU-bound work.',
      },
      {
        q:       'You scale to 100 workers, each opening 5 Postgres connections. What problem do you encounter, and how do you fix it?',
        options: [
          'Workers run out of memory — add more RAM to each worker',
          'Postgres connection limit exceeded — add a connection pooler like PgBouncer',
          'Redis becomes a bottleneck — switch to a faster cache',
          'Workers conflict on writes — add database transactions',
        ],
        answer:  1,
        explain: '100 workers × 5 connections = 500 simultaneous Postgres connections, which typically exceeds the default limit (100). PgBouncer sits in front of Postgres and multiplexes many application connections into a smaller pool of real database connections, solving this without changing application code.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Which metric should you monitor to decide when to scale agent workers, and why is CPU a poor choice?',
        options: [
          'CPU utilisation — high CPU means workers are overloaded',
          'Memory usage — memory exhaustion causes crashes',
          'Queue depth and queue latency — these directly measure how much work is waiting',
          'Network bandwidth — agents transfer lots of data',
        ],
        answer:  2,
        explain: 'Agent workers idle on CPU while waiting on LLM and API calls — CPU will look low even when workers are completely saturated with I/O. Queue depth (how many tasks are waiting) and queue latency (how long tasks wait before starting) directly measure whether you need more workers.',
      },
    ]} />

    <DeepDive
      sources="§4.15"
      tensions={[
        { a: 'More workers = higher throughput', b: 'More workers = higher LLM cost' },
        { a: 'TTLs on session state (cost control)', b: 'TTLs too short (loses long-running session context)' },
        { a: 'Fair scheduling (equality)', b: 'Priority lanes (premium tier SLA)' },
      ]}
      questions={[
        {
          q:      'A tenant is running a legitimate bulk processing job that is consuming 80% of your worker pool, slowing everyone else down. You have concurrency limits in place, but they are set per-task, not per-tenant. How do you fix this without rejecting the legitimate work?',
          hint:   'Think about shaping traffic rather than blocking it.',
          answer: '<p>The issue is per-task concurrency limits don\'t account for tenant-level total usage. Solutions: (1) <strong>Per-tenant concurrency limit</strong> — a Redis counter that caps how many tasks this specific tenant can run simultaneously (e.g. 5), regardless of total pool size; (2) <strong>Weighted fair queuing</strong> — instead of round-robin per task, rotate per-tenant: give each active tenant a turn before any tenant gets a second slot; (3) <strong>Priority lanes</strong> — the bulk job goes into a lower-priority queue that only workers with nothing else to do will pick up; premium/interactive tasks have a higher-priority queue that workers always prefer. The bulk work completes, just more slowly, without starving other tenants. Do not reject the work — that breaks the user\'s legitimate use case and erodes trust.</p>',
        },
        {
          q:      'You want to use TTLs on Redis session state to auto-clean abandoned sessions. What are the risks of setting TTLs too short or too long, and how do you choose the right value?',
          hint:   'Consider the range of legitimate agent task durations in your system.',
          answer: '<p>Too short: a session actively running a long task (research agent, multi-step planning) gets evicted while mid-execution. The next worker fetches null context and either crashes or starts over from scratch — wasted work and a bad user experience. Too long: abandoned sessions (user closed the browser, network disconnected) accumulate indefinitely, consuming Redis memory without ever being accessed again. Choosing the right TTL: (1) Profile your p99 task duration — if 99% of tasks finish in under 30 minutes, a 60-minute TTL is safe; (2) Implement a "heartbeat" pattern — the worker writes a keep-alive to the key every 30 seconds, extending the TTL as long as the task is active; abandoned sessions naturally expire when heartbeats stop; (3) Use separate TTLs by task type — quick chatbot sessions (5 minutes) vs. deep research agents (4 hours).</p>',
        },
      ]}
      pitfalls={[
        'Sticky sessions — routing a user to the same worker every time creates a single point of failure and defeats horizontal scaling.',
        'Scaling workers without scaling the database connection pool — Postgres will start refusing connections long before your workers feel the squeeze.',
        'Setting worker count to match peak traffic rather than rate-limit budget — you end up spending 10× on LLM calls during a traffic spike.',
        'Storing large tool outputs (PDFs, scraped pages) in Redis — hot storage is for fast-access ephemeral context, not large blobs; use S3 references instead.',
        'Graceful shutdown without a drain timeout — workers that accept SIGTERM immediately drop in-flight tasks; always complete the current step before terminating.',
      ]}
    />

  </div>

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>

